nit_claim": true}}], "viewerUrl": "https://us.cloud.langfuse.com/project/cmmxymjnc032zad07j35swt3h/traces/5f768a9427866622ab86b3a6bbfdc26f", "ts": "2026-09-29T00:37:15.106Z"}, "id": 3, "domain": "VFD / Variable Frequency Drive Faults & Operation", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "scenario", "correct_answer": "A", "model_answer": "A", "parse_rule": "letter_on_own_line", "is_correct": true, "http": 200, "turn_status": "answered", "basis": "general_reasoning", "trace_id": "5f768a9427866622ab86b3a6bbfdc26f", "response_time_ms": 6652, "answer_text": "⚠️ **Energized equipment.** Any step below that restores power or takes a reading on live conductors is energized work: qualified person, energized-work permit, arc-flash assessment and the PPE it specifies (NFPA 70E). Where you can, read the value from the drive, MCC metering or a power monitor instead of opening the enclosure.\n\nA\n\nThe first thing to check is the drive’s input voltage – ensure the 460 V supply is present and within tolerance before looking at internal settings.  \n\n1. De‑energize, lockout/tagout the VFD and verify the input terminals are dead.  \n2. Re‑energize only the supply side (with proper PPE) and measure the line voltage at the drive’s input terminals.  \n3. If the input is low or missing, correct the supply issue; then re‑measure the output voltage.  \n\nIf the input voltage is correct, the next checks would be carrier frequency, output current limit, and finally confirming the motor’s nameplate voltage."}
